# The Fundamental Human Assets

2026-09-19

A certain experience from some years ago wrenched me from a trance I had caught myself in. In shock I wandered the arid back hills of my father's community, and the ideas written here came to me over the course of that walk as I gazed down at the Okanagan Lakes. I think the pain granted me momentary clarity.

I realized that we have confused the Moon for its reflection, the thing itself for its abstraction, actual possession for Money its proxy. This is not to say that Money does not exist, for we can define existence as follows:

> If it affects you, it exists.

Under this definition, a thing need not be present in the physical, visible world for it to exist in a meaningful sense. Does trust exist? I should say so; to assume the alternative would be misery.

Money, through powerful people concerned with it, can absolutely alter your life in ways beyond your control. Thus Money at least deserves your attention, but as I will outline, it should not receive your obsession. While we can't take anything with us to the other side - save perhaps our own souls - I believe there is value in understanding our True Assets for the time that we are here alive.

## The Five Assets

The fundamental assets which every human possesses are these:

* Focus
* Health
* Connections
* Space
* Dignity

We can exchange one for another, or for Money and back again. Here, Money is a kind of vapour, an in-between energy that flows between the real five but is quick to disappear. Much of the activity of a human life involves itself in the procurement and management of these five, and to the degree that we have them, perhaps that's where happiness is.

Let us look at each one in detail.

### Focus

For years within my private writings this first asset was called Time. However recents readings and reflection have convinced me that Time should not be considered something we have but something we are. We may think:

> I spent two weeks fixing up my house.

as if we had traded those preallocated two weeks of lifespan for an improved Space, but as we cannot know our date of departure, it is impossible to measure our supposed allotment of Time as we can for the others. Therefore let us instead consider Focus, a concrete thing we do have which can be parceled out in service of the others.

Your Focus is your attention. Every adult understands painfully how little of this we seem to have to spare, and it is perhaps the main bottleneck on free transfer between the categories. For instance, a job is a simple allocation of Focus for Money, but as you can't allocate infinite Focus, you can't receive infinite Money over any span of time.

Assets can be combined during exchange: Focus and Money can be drained for a gym membership, improving your Health. In the home example above, it wasn't your Time that you traded but your Focus, perhaps neglecting other activities that may have improved your Connections (say, a party you skipped). Likewise, what we call burnout is a different interplay between Focus, Health, and Money, which drains Health to compensate for an over-allocation of Focus.

When we say "I don't have time" what we really mean is "I don't have capacity", and while we can't make Time as we maybe think we can, freeing our Focus is well within reach. Measuring one's Focus isn't as easy as looking at a bank account, but we do have a natural signal from our bodies: stress. If you're feeling overwhelmed, you've probably over-allocated, and as above the cost will likely be your Health.

### Health

You are alive, and your body has a biological and structural state. This state degrades over time but can be actively improved, in fact far beyond the local minimum that the body seems to settle in in the life of a city-dweller. If through neglect or sudden accident your Health reaches its "zero", you are dead. Even the experience of hunger is a temporary drop in Health, such that for wage workers the world over, the core loop of life becomes the allocation of Focus for Money (a job) and then Money for Health (food and shelter), which in turn grants us another day of Focus to repeat the cycle.

How do we measure our Health? We visit the doctor. We step on the weigh scale. We look at our skin and hair in the mirror. We reflect on how it feels to wake up every morning. We time ourselves at the race track and record our weight lifting records. More of this is always better than less.

### Connections

Your family and friends are your Connections. As social creatures, we seem to need these. A state of solitary hermitage may be adapted to, but it is not the default. We are not cats. Professional associations may also count, but I somehow doubt that Hiroki-san from management is going to help you move apartments on Saturday.

Are Connections about getting things from other people? While the Connections themselves seem to hold intrinsic value, they also enhance the exchange of the other Assets even within oneself. Be honest: how fun is it to see a movie, play billiards, or go fishing on your own?

We find here also a new formulation of Introversion and Extroversion: the extrovert naturally allocates Focus to Connections, while the introvert chooses other avenues.

A few more examples:

* Having a family grounds many people.
* The artist in the woods trades Connections for Focus.
* An imbalanced parent may sacrifice as much as they can for their children, particularly Health. But if you die, what then?

### Space

It may not feel like it when living in the center of a metropolis, but the Earth is mostly empty. There is room enough for all of us, and carving out a piece of it for oneself is a natural expectation. Your Space begins in the air immediately outside your body. How close is the nearest human to you? Are they there by your choice, and are you happy about it? The opposite situation is perhaps easier to imagine: a crowded morning subway. To the degree that you do not have control over your immediate air, you are poor in Space.

Next is your home. I do not know the ideal size, but as I grew up in a house I suspect that's better than a jail cell. Speaking of jail, to be incarcerated and lack freedom of movement, or simply to not possess a passport makes you poorer in Space than you could be. Expanding this thinking, cars and private jets are a form of Space. Even horses, as riding one can bring you both physically and mentally to a place happily unique from one's daily bipedal experience.

Real Estate agents and property developers are people whose entire profession centers around the exchange of Money for Space. To place a criminal in solitary confinement is to deny them both their Connections and their Space, and we consider this one of the worst ways to punish someone.

It should also be clear that we mean something significant when we say that introverts often have a "rich inner world".

### Dignity

We could call this also "Decency", "Goodness", "Humanity", or "Soul". It is a trait unique to conscious creatures, and is why it is wrong to say that humans are just another animal. It is what enabled Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn to bear the gulags and what is diminished when we say someone is broken or has "sold out".

Someone rich in Dignity would appear ideal to the clergy.

> "Yes, that is what we meant."

While there are any number of professions and Entertainments that trade Dignity for something else. The exhaustion of Dignity may not kill you physically, but if it cripples your personal story, is that not just as bad? To counteract these ever-present drains on Dignity is one of the reasons why religions exist in the first place. To be cynical of them as houses of control is to miss that point.

Like Focus, Dignity can only be measured indirectly, and like Health the mirror may be a good starting point.

## Other Considerations

### Money

I have named Money a gas as to highlight its volatility. Consider:

* A government can freeze your bank accounts.
* Bank runs demonstrate that even your stated balance isn't fully there.
* A shift in exchange rates or runaway inflation can plummet the value of your savings.
* Wallets can be lost or stolen.
* Debt exists because people have trouble moderating their spending.

Money is only as real as the true thing it becomes upon exchange, and only in the moment of that becoming. Were you to hoard a lot of it and lose it before transforming it, then you never had anything at all. We know this of course, which is why we have invented a variety of forms in which to encode Money in attempts to counteract its tendency to disappear. In descending order of realness:

* Cash in your hand.
* Precious metals. Have to be resold before use and can still be stolen.
* Cash in your bank. Weak to bank runs, but balances are usually insured by the federal government.
* Bonds. Money you've lent to the government to be repaid with interest after a locked period.
* Stocks. An IOU with a floating market value. Only as real as your ability to resell. Evaporates if the company fails.
* Credit. Money borrowed through time from your future self. Disappears if the bank or card company cuts you off.
* Crypocurrency. Mostly not usable as-is. Easily hacked, lost, or scammed away. Almost entirely unregulated.

But is not the best option to transfer your Money back out into the Five True Assets whenever possible? The pursuit of Money itself is only meaningful if the transformation target of that Money is meaningful.

### Possessions

What is a book? Clothing? A watch? Cutlery? It depends on its function and why you bought it.

Many people shop as Entertainment - not because they need something, but because the acquirement makes them feel good. If the object is eventually thrown out, it was equivalent to a temporary pleasure like a movie. If eventually resold, it was a crude store of value.

### Wisdom: The Hyper Asset

There is a secret sixth asset: Wisdom. It differs from the others in that no one can take it away from you, and it doesn't diminish upon exchange. The other assets (save perhaps Money) can buy you Wisdom, and that Wisdom can in turn lead back to the others but without itself being lost. In this sense, the accumulation and spread of Wisdom is a true Positive-Sum game.

### Entertainment

Leisure seems necessary for a balanced human experience. Like the assets, there are some fundamental categories of Entertainment:

* Stories (spoken, children's "pretend play", books, movies, video games)
* Sport
* Food
* Music
* Travel or similar "exploration"
* Sex

We must entertain ourselves, despite the fact that this almost always drains one of the fundamental assets (or Money) with nothing permanent in return. It simply keeps life going. This acts as a natural check on the over-accumulation of the assets, but also leads to addictions and life distractions when we seek too much of it.

Examples of trades:

* Focus => Playing games.
* Health => Smoking, drugs, alcohol.
* Connections => Promiscuity.
* Space => House parties, riding an airplane.
* Dignity => I'm sure you can imagine.

Gambling too is perhaps the most direct way to burn Money for pleasure. I don't know what the optimal amount of Entertainment is, but in my case restlessness is a sign that I'm getting too much.

## Final Thoughts

I hope these words and lists will help you reflect on your priorities and the flow of energy through your daily life. As for me, I will not soon forget that afternoon in the dusty hills, although it would be nice is Wisdom came more readily.

